  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with a trough along the West Coast with \nthe associated low tracking east along the Washington/BC Border and \na broad high pressure over Texas pulling deep monsoonal moisture up \nthrough Arizona into eastern Utah and Western Colorado keeping pwats \nover an inch, and keeping the jet in place southwest to northeast \nacross the Western States. Though most of the energy across the \nnorth was spent on the afternoon thunderstorms, upper-level dynamics \nare keeping showers in the forecast with occasional lightning \novernight. The moisture stays in the area into Tuesday before drier \nair pushes in from the northwest. \n\nThe jet is dipping south across the region to support the convection \nwe had Saturday across the northern areas spreading south of I-70 to \nthe San Juans Sunday. A shortwave trough drops down Sunday afternoon \ninto the evening bringing a round of stronger thunderstorms with \nnocturnal storms into Monday. A second shortwave is threatening a \nrepeat Monday afternoon and evening, but models are diverging on the \ntiming, so confidence is lower at this point. Stay tuned for \nupdates. The primary threats are flash flooding due to heavy rain \nand training storms, especially near recent fire scars, possible \nlarge hail, and 60 mph outflow winds as is typical with severe \nthunderstorms. Though the air will be saturated to the surface, \nexpect strong winds due to the wet downbursts mixing down the 40 mph \nflow at H500. \n\nModels further diverge in outcomes by Wednesday leading to \nrather low forecast confidence midweek and beyond. There still \nremains a signal for another push of monsoonal moisture late \nnext week. This moisture could also be enhanced by a tropical \nsystem situated in the eastern Pacific, which we will have to \nsee evolve to gain a clearer picture. As for temperatures, a \nwelcome relief is in store as daytime highs are expected to\nremain below normal through mid week.",
